// The 4-byte scan total (ADR-0006, Wave D / D3).
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. The greedy mesher's emit pass writes quads at
// exclusive-scanned offsets, so the number it actually produced is
// Offsets[MaskCount-1] + Counts[MaskCount-1]. That derivation is trivial — and
// today it happens on the CPU, which is why the WHOLE Counts and Offsets
// arrays are read back (2 x MaskCount x 4 bytes, ~24 KB for a render chunk)
// purely to recover 4 bytes of information. The quad buffer then comes back
// too, at its 32-quads-per-mask upper bound, because nothing knows how much of
// it is live until those arrays have landed:
//
//   MaskCount 3,072 -> MaxQuads 98,304 -> 786 KB read back per chunk,
//   against a typical ~900-1,500 live quads, i.e. ~7-12 KB.
//
// A 65-100x over-read on every chunk, and ~6.4 GB of PCIe traffic to fill R0
// once at 128 m against a kernel time measured at ~0.09 s. On that path it is
// the readback, not the GPU, that sets streaming throughput.
//
// So the total is computed HERE, on the GPU, into a one-element buffer, and
// that is the only thing frame N reads back.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT IN worldgen.ush, WHICH IS WHERE THE SCAN LIVES. Two reasons,
// both about blast radius rather than tidiness:
//
//   1. The determinism gate compiles worldgen.ush two ways and pins a digest
//      over the result. This kernel has nothing to do with worldgen and no
//      business inside that contract; putting it here means it cannot move the
//      digest, cannot require a SPIR-V respin, and cannot fail the shader lint
//      for reasons unrelated to world generation.
//   2. worldgen.ush is the file ground rule 10 is about: it is shared with the
//      main checkout that other waves run editors out of, and editing a .ush
//      under a live editor kills the whole shader compile batch.
//
// VoxelQuadDecodeTest.usf is the precedent for a small kernel living project-
// side rather than in voxel-core.

#include "/Engine/Public/Platform.ush"

StructuredBuffer<uint> InQuadCounts;
StructuredBuffer<uint> InQuadOffsets;
RWStructuredBuffer<uint> OutQuadTotal;   // exactly one element
uint MaskCount;

// One thread. The work is two loads and an add; a wider dispatch would only
// add ways for it to be wrong.
[numthreads(1, 1, 1)]
void QuadTotalMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	if (tid.x != 0)
	{
		return;
	}

	// A zero-mask region has no interior brick to mesh, so it emits nothing.
	// ValidateRegionRequest already rejects that shape, but reading
	// InQuadCounts[MaskCount - 1] with MaskCount == 0 would underflow to
	// 0xFFFFFFFF and index wildly out of bounds -- the exact class of fault
	// tools/lint-shader-ub.py exists to prevent, so it is guarded here even
	// though the host cannot currently produce it.
	if (MaskCount == 0)
	{
		OutQuadTotal[0] = 0;
		return;
	}

	// The scan is EXCLUSIVE, so the last mask's start offset plus its own
	// count is the total. Identical derivation to the CPU one this replaces
	// (VoxelGpuWorldGen.cpp's RunRegionBlocking and the async manager's
	// harvest), deliberately, so the two can be cross-checked.
	const uint Last = MaskCount - 1;
	OutQuadTotal[0] = InQuadOffsets[Last] + InQuadCounts[Last];
}
